* Filamentary ReRAM: SET and RESET in a triangular sweep, at two ambient temperatures.
*
* Two cells of models/multiphysics/reram/reram.va, each behind a 2 kOhm series resistor (the
* current compliance), driven by the same 0 -> +1.6 -> 0 -> -1.6 -> 0 V triangle over 10 ms.
* X1 sits at 300 K, X2 at 400 K. Each cell's gap starts open (1.7 nm, high resistance).
*
* What to look for (the cells report SET/RESET with V, I, gap and filament temperature; the
* figures are measured on X1 at 2.14.0+3):
*   1. SET starts abruptly but is self-limiting: once the gap starts closing, more current
*      heats the filament and speeds the drift, but the 2 kOhm resistor takes a growing share of
*      the voltage, so the cell's own voltage falls -- negative feedback, the current compliance.
*      The gap closes from 1.5 to 0.3 nm over 0.58 ms of the ramp (SET reported at 0.641 V cell
*      voltage, filament at 413 K).
*   2. RESET is the abrupt one: it starts only once the low-resistance cell carries enough
*      current to heat its filament to ~610 K (reported at -0.830 V), and as the gap opens the
*      voltage moves from the resistor onto the cell -- positive feedback. The gap reopens from
*      0.3 to 1.5 nm in 10.5 us, 1/700 of the ramp.
*   3. The hotter cell switches earlier: X2 (400 K ambient) SETs at 0.507 V and RESETs at
*      -0.696 V, because exp(-Ea/kT) at its starting temperature is 1,412 times the 300 K value
*      (Ea = 0.75 eV).
*   4. The I-V loop is pinched: I = 0 whenever V = 0, whatever the state (I ~ sinh(V/v0)), the
*      memristor fingerprint (docs/examples/multiphysics/reram_iv.py draws it).
* Run: va-cli sim circuits/multiphysics/reram_sweep.net --model models/multiphysics/reram --tran --report X1.g,X2.g --plot docs/examples/multiphysics/reram_gap.svg
V1 in gnd PWL(0 0 2.5m 1.6 5m 0 7.5m -1.6 10m 0)
R1 in te1 2k
X1 te1 gnd reram tamb=300
R2 in te2 2k
X2 te2 gnd reram tamb=400
.tran 1u 10m
.end
